{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"golden-convergent-boutu","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe lexical tier reaches semantic recall@5 0.823. ADR 0122 measured one candidate that beats it distinguishably at k=5: `vector` — a Haiku-written digest of each ticket, embedded and ranked by cosine similarity — at 0.876, McNemar p=0.0213, recovering 13 pairs and losing 3. That single comparison is the entire justification for this tier, and it is worth 93 otherwise-invisible duplicate pairs on this store.\n\nThe tier must be optional and vendor-neutral. rebar runs against diverse model providers and against none, so retrieval that is reachable only through one vendor's endpoint would make the upgrade unavailable to most installations and would fail the blocking `project.portability` criterion.\n\n## Approach\n\n**No new provider abstraction.** pydantic-ai 1.107.9 — already rebar's LLM runtime behind `src/rebar/llm/providers.py` — ships a first-class embeddings API: `Embedder`, `EmbeddingModel`, `EmbeddingResult`, `EmbeddingSettings`, `infer_embedding_model` and `KnownEmbeddingModelName`, with provider backends for `bedrock`, `openai`, `cohere`, `google`, `voyageai` and `sentence_transformers`, plus `OpenAIEmbeddingsCompatibleProvider` for arbitrary OpenAI-compatible endpoints. Dispatching a `provider:model` string through `infer_embedding_model` is the same shape `providers.py` already uses for chat models, so this tier is a configured model name over an existing seam, not a new one.\n\nThat seam settles portability concretely rather than by intent: `sentence_transformers` runs locally with no credentials and no network, so an air-gapped installation can run the embedding tier; `TestEmbeddingModel` makes the tier deterministic in tests without mocking a vendor's wire format.\n\n**Configured default.** `bedrock:amazon.titan-embed-text-v2:0`, 1,024 dimensions, cosine — the model ADR 0122 measured. It is a default, not a requirement: an installation that cannot reach it configures another model, and one that configures none runs the lexical tier, which remains fully functional.\n\n**Digest step.** `bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0`, the 4-field prompt pinned at commit `f147e08242f`, input capped at 40,000 characters, per ADR 0122's architecture section. The digest is kept despite being the weakest link in the chain, and the plan states why rather than hiding it: `vector` is *not* distinguishable from `vector-raw`, which skips the digest (p=0.0654 and p=0.0625). The digest survives only because `vector-raw` also fails to beat `fts5` (p=0.6636) — a non-transitive chain ADR 0122 states plainly. Dropping the digest therefore cannot be justified from the current evidence, and neither can a strong claim that it helps.\n\n**Two counters ADR 0122 makes mandatory for any consumer.** Its Consequences section records that the digest parser silently returned empty fields for three distinct reply shapes, each indistinguishable from success without an `empties` counter; and that 2 of 630 tickets did not answer the task at all, both succeeding on retry, which makes retry the correct handling rather than a workaround. Both are carried here as acceptance criteria because a consumer that omits them reproduces a defect already diagnosed.\n\n**Ranking swaps behind an interface that already has a working implementation.** The index, dir-hash freshness, read-phase catch-up and three-surface advisory all come from the lexical story; this tier replaces only the scoring function and adds the digest-and-embed build step. k stays at 5 — ADR 0122's decision is scoped to that operating point, and at k=10 `vector`'s semantic edge is indistinguishable (p=0.3438).\n\n**Degradation is the default behaviour, not an error path.** When no embedding model is configured, when the provider denies or errors, when the digest parse yields empty fields, or when the embedding index is stale, retrieval answers from the lexical tier with a diagnostic and the create still succeeds. This is what makes the tier safe to enable.\n\nAny ratcheted mechanism this tier adds — the embedding-model config key is the expected one — carries a `# mechanism-ok:` marker at its definition site naming this ticket, since `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` fails on any unadmitted new mechanism.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An embedding model configured as a `provider:model` string is resolved through pydantic-ai's `infer_embedding_model`, and retrieval ranks by cosine similarity over the resulting vectors. Checked by: a test that configures `TestEmbeddingModel` and asserts the returned ordering follows cosine similarity rather than the lexical score.\n- [ ] The tier runs end to end against at least two different providers without code change, one of which requires no credentials and no network. Checked by: a test parameterized over `sentence_transformers` and `TestEmbeddingModel` asserting both produce ranked results from the same store.\n- [ ] With no embedding model configured, retrieval returns the lexical tier's results and the create exits 0. Checked by: a test asserting the advisory matches the lexical tier's output when the key is unset.\n- [ ] When the configured provider raises a denial, when it times out, and when the digest reply parses to empty fields, retrieval falls back to lexical-only with a diagnostic and the create exits 0. Checked by: one test per failure mode asserting exit status, advisory contents and the diagnostic.\n- [ ] An `empties` counter records every digest reply that parses to an empty field, and is observable after an index build. Checked by: a test feeding each of the three reply shapes ADR 0122 records and asserting the counter equals 3 while the build still completes.\n- [ ] A digest reply that does not answer the task is retried, and a success on retry is recorded as a success. Checked by: a test whose model fails once then succeeds, asserting the ticket is indexed and the retry is counted.\n- [ ] Every module added or modified under `src/rebar/` is at most 800 lines, and `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` both exit 0, with any new mechanism admitted by a `# mechanism-ok:` marker naming this ticket. Checked by: running the two scripts and `make verify`.","id":"2041-75d6-2a2f-4be9","parent_id":"4e89-30b9-1666-4e53","priority":2,"tags":["complexity-reduction"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Add an opt-in embedding retrieval tier over pydantic-ai's provider-agnostic embeddings seam"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793578986361911586,"uuid":"730c2562-bf00-4439-b5b0-d171539f446b"}